-   141→   141→          Two-factor authentication uses TOTP (RFC 6238) — the same standard as Google Authenticator, Authy,
-   142→   142→          1Password. Setup happens in two phases: first you scan the QR code and verify a real code (only then is
-   143→   143→          the secret persisted), then every future login requires both password and a fresh 6-digit code. Disabling
-   144→   144→          2FA requires your password; an admin can reset it (which also clears the secret).
-   145→   145→
-   146→   146→          TRY IT
-   147→   147→          1. Sign in as registrar@example.com.
-   148→   148→          2. Open the user menu (top-right) → "Account Security".
-   149→   149→          3. Click "Enable 2FA" → scan the QR code with an authenticator app.
-   150→   150→          4. Enter the 6-digit code from your app to confirm.
-   151→   151→          5. Sign out, then sign back in — you'll be prompted for a 2FA code after your password.
-   152→   152→
-   153→   153→              WHAT TO LOOK FOR
-   154→   154→
-   155→   155→          → The QR code is generated server-side from a per-user secret stored in the DB; the secret is never sent back to the
-   156→   156→              client in plaintext after setup.
-   157→   157→
-   158→   158→          → After enabling, the login flow has TWO steps: password → 2FA challenge → authenticated session. The 2FA challenge
-   159→   159→              is a short-lived opaque token (30s), not the session itself.
-   160→   160→
-   161→   161→          → A wrong 2FA code increments the SAME lockout counter as a wrong password — 3 wrong attempts of any kind locks
-   162→   162→              the account.
